  "worked_solution": "The function $p(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $p(2)=2$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}p(x)=1$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}p(x)\\ne p(2)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=2,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=2$ but they are *not* removable.",
